#{[17592186055013 "Implicit differentiation gives $2x+8y\\,y'=0$, so $y'=-x/(4y)$ wherever $y\\ne0$. At $(\\sqrt2,1)$, the slope is $$-\\frac{\\sqrt2}{4}=-\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}.$$"] [17592186055014 "Implicit differentiation gives the tangent slope $-x/(4y)=-1/(2\\sqrt2)$ at $(\\sqrt2,1)$. The normal slope is its negative reciprocal, $2\\sqrt2$. Thus the normal is $y-1=2\\sqrt2(x-\\sqrt2)$. Setting $y=0$ yields $$x=\\sqrt2-\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}=\\frac3{2\\sqrt2}.$$ Therefore $P=(\\tfrac3{2\\sqrt2},0)$."] [17592186055017 "Differentiating gives $6x-4y\\,y'=0$, hence the tangent slope at $(2,2)$ is $3/2$. The normal slope is $-2/3$, so its equation is $y-2=-\\tfrac23(x-2)$. At $x=0$, $y=2+\\tfrac43=\\tfrac{10}3$. Thus $P=(0,\\tfrac{10}3)$."] [17592186055138 "Differentiate implicitly: $3x^2+3y^2y'=0$. Hence $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x^2}{y^2}$$ wherever the curve has a finite derivative, namely $y\\ne0$."] [17592186055139 "Differentiate implicitly: $$-\\sin y\\,y'+4x-y'=10.$$ Solving for the derivative gives $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4x-10}{1+\\sin y}=\\frac{2(2x-5)}{\\sin y+1},$$ where the denominator is nonzero."] [17592186055140 "Apply the product and chain rules: $y+xy'+2yy'=6x$. Thus $$(x+2y)y'=6x-y\\implies\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6x-y}{x+2y},$$ where $x+2y\\ne0$."] [17592186059528 "We have\n\n$$\\overset{\\to}{OA} = a = i + 3j,; \\overset{\\to}{OB} = b = 2i - 2j$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\overset{\\to}{AB} &= \\overset{\\to}{OB} - \\overset{\\to}{OA} \\\\\n&= b - a \\\\\n&= (2i - 2j) - (i + 3j) \\\\\n&= i - 5j\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\overset{\\to}{AB} \\mid &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (-5)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{26}\n\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186059530 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}|}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2+2 \\\\ 0+(-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a + 2b \\mid &= \\sqrt{0^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186059959 "The strict inequalities exclude both endpoints. Use parentheses at both ends: $x\\in(3,7)$."] [17592186060012 "The lower endpoint $9$ is excluded, and the upper endpoint $13$ is included. Thus $x\\in(9,13]$."] [17592186060029 "Use the Euclidean norm: $$\\|v\\|=\\sqrt{4^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{17}.$$"] [17592186060119 "First $a+3b=\\langle-1,0\\rangle+3\\langle3,-2\\rangle=\\langle8,-6\\rangle$. Its norm is $10$. Dividing by this positive norm preserves direction and gives the unit vector $\\langle4/5,-3/5\\rangle$."] [17592186060126 "Use the Euclidean norm: $$\\|a\\|=\\sqrt{(-12)^2+3^2}=\\sqrt{153}=3\\sqrt{17}.$$"] [17592186060127 "The displacement is $\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle3-(-4),0-1\\rangle=\\langle7,-1\\rangle$. Its length is $$\\sqrt{7^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{50}=5\\sqrt2.$$"] [17592186060128 "The displacement is $\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\langle3-(-2),3-2\\rangle=\\langle5,1\\rangle$. Its length is $$\\sqrt{5^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{26}.$$"] [17592186060129 "The norm of $a$ is $\\sqrt{3^2+8^2}=\\sqrt{73}$. Dividing by this positive number preserves direction and gives $$\\frac{a}{\\|a\\|}=\\begin{bmatrix}3/\\sqrt{73}\\\\8/\\sqrt{73}\\end{bmatrix}.$$"] [17592186060252 "The square bracket includes $-5$ and the parenthesis excludes $2\\sqrt2$. Therefore $-5\\le y<2\\sqrt2$."] [17592186060264 "The open circle at $-1$ excludes that endpoint, and the filled circle at $2$ includes that endpoint. The segment represents $x\\in(-1,2]$."] [17592186060267 "Both endpoint circles, at $1$ and $4$, are open. The segment represents $x\\in(1,4)$."] [17592186060310 "Both parentheses exclude their endpoints. Therefore $x\\in(3,4)$ means $3<x<4$."] [17592186060311 "Both square brackets include their endpoints. Therefore $x\\in[-2,1]$ means $-2\\le x\\le1$."] [17592186069083 "Use the slope formula: $$m=\\frac{3-5}{2-4}=\\frac{-2}{-2}=1.$$"] [17592186069195 "The intercepts are $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$. Hence $$m=\\frac{0-4}{2-0}=-2.$$"] [17592186069343 "The plotted line is horizontal: its $y$-coordinate stays constant as $x$ changes. Its rise is zero, so its slope is $0$."] [17592186069344 "The plotted line is vertical. Any two distinct points on it have zero horizontal change, so the slope would require division by zero. The slope is undefined."] [17592186069348 "The plotted points are $(1,0)$ and $(3,6)$. Thus $$m=\\frac{6-0}{3-1}=3.$$"] [17592186069451 "The intercepts are $(-4,0)$ and $(0,2)$. Hence $$m=\\frac{2-0}{0-(-4)}=\\frac12.$$"] [17592186069452 "The line falls as we move from left to right. Thus its rise is negative for a positive run, and its slope is negative."] [17592186069511 "The line rises as we move from left to right. Thus its rise and run have the same sign, and its slope is positive."] [17592186073446 "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n\n$$\\qquad\\:\\:\\:\n\\begin{cases}{\\color{red}2u+9v-6=0}\\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}(1)} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2u+6v-8=0}\\hspace{2em}{\\color{blue}(2)}\\end{cases}.$$\n\n\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\\\n2u+6v &= 8 \\\\\n2u &= 8 - 6v \\\\\nu &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\\\nu &= 4 - 3v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n2+3v &= 0 \\\\\n3v &= -2 \\\\\nv &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-12 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u &= 12 \\\\\n\tu &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$, which can be written as $\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right).$"] [17592186073997 "The first equation gives $q=3p-9$. Substitute into the second equation: $$5p+4(3p-9)=32\\implies17p=68\\implies p=4.$$ Hence $q=3(4)-9=3$. Substitution into both original equations gives $-9$ and $32$, as required."] [17592186073998 "The second equation gives $b=-a$. Substitute into the first equation: $$-7a+2(-a)=18\\implies-9a=18\\implies a=-2.$$ Then $b=2$. Indeed, $-7(-2)+2(2)=18$ and $6(-2)+6(2)=0$."] [17592186074030 "Substitute $y=x+5$ into the first equation: $$x+5(x+5)=13\\implies6x=-12\\implies x=-2.$$ Then $y=-2+5=3$. Both original equations hold, so the ordered solution is $(-2,3)$."] [17592186074047 "From $5q+55=0$, we obtain $q=-11$. Substituting into the second equation gives $2p-33+3=0$, so $p=15$. Therefore $p-q=15-(-11)=26$."] [17592186074172 "The second equation gives $b=2+4a$. Substitute into the first equation: $3a+2(2+4a)=4$, so $11a=0$. Thus $a=0$, $b=2$, and $a-b=-2$."] [17592186088018 "The center is the midpoint of the diameter: $((-4+2)/2,(2+2)/2)=(-1,2)$. The diameter has length $6$, so the radius is $3$. Thus $$(x+1)^2+(y-2)^2=9.$$"] [17592186088019 "The center is the midpoint of $(6,4)$ and $(-4,2)$, namely $(1,3)$. The squared diameter is $10^2+2^2=104$, so the squared radius is $104/4=26$. Hence $$(x-1)^2+(y-3)^2=26.$$"] [17592186088205 "The diagram gives center $(1,3)$ and radius $3$. Substitute into $(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2$ to obtain $$(x-1)^2+(y-3)^2=9.$$"] [17592186088208 "The diagram gives center $(-2,5)$ and radius $7$. Therefore $$(x+2)^2+(y-5)^2=49.$$"] [17592186088292 "The squared radius is the squared distance from $C(-1,2)$ to $X(2,3)$: $r^2=(2+1)^2+(3-2)^2=10$. Hence the circle is $$(x+1)^2+(y-2)^2=10.$$"] [17592186088312 "Compare $(x+5)^2+(y+1)^2=4$ with $(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2$. The center is $(-5,-1)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt4=2$."] [17592186092573 "For a parametric curve, $$L=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{(x')^2+(y')^2}\\,dt.$$ Here $x'=4\\cos4t$ and $y'=-4\\sin t$. Therefore $$L=4\\int_0^2\\sqrt{\\cos^2(4t)+\\sin^2t}\\,dt.$$"] [17592186092574 "Differentiate: $x'=2t$ and $y'=3t^2+2$. Thus $$(x')^2+(y')^2=4t^2+(3t^2+2)^2=9t^4+16t^2+4.$$ Therefore $$L=\\int_{-1}^1\\sqrt{9t^4+16t^2+4}\\,dt.$$"] [17592186092597 "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. With $r=1$ and $\\theta=5\\pi/3$, the coordinates are $(\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2)$."] [17592186092703 "The radius of $A$ is $r=\\sqrt{10+10}=2\\sqrt5$. Thus $B$ has radius $2r=4\\sqrt5$ and angle $\\pi/3$, giving $$B=(4\\sqrt5\\cos(\\pi/3),4\\sqrt5\\sin(\\pi/3))=(2\\sqrt5,2\\sqrt{15}).$$"] [17592186092704 "The coordinates of $A$ give $\\cos\\theta=\\sqrt2/2$ and $\\sin\\theta=-\\sqrt2/2$, so $\\theta=-\\pi/4$ modulo $2\\pi$. The angle of $B$ is $-\\pi/2$ modulo $2\\pi$, and its radius is $1$. Therefore $B=(0,-1)$."] [17592186092705 "The diagram puts $B$ counterclockwise from $A$ in the second quadrant. Its angle is $\\pi/4+5\\pi/12=2\\pi/3$ and its radius is $8$. Therefore $$B=(8\\cos(2\\pi/3),8\\sin(2\\pi/3))=(-4,4\\sqrt3).$$"] [17592186092706 "The diagram puts $B$ clockwise from $A$ in the second quadrant. Its angle is $210^\\circ-75^\\circ=135^\\circ$ and its radius is $6$. Thus $$B=(6\\cos135^\\circ,6\\sin135^\\circ)=(-3\\sqrt2,3\\sqrt2).$$"] [17592186092826 "Differentiate: $x'=2t+2$, $y'=2t^2-2$. Their squared sum is $$4(t+1)^2+4(t^2-1)^2=4(t^4-t^2+2t+2).$$ Hence $$L=2\\int_{-3}^2\\sqrt{t^4-t^2+2t+2}\\,dt.$$ The square root keeps the speed nonnegative on the entire interval, including where $t+1$ changes sign."] [17592186092856 "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. With $r=4$ and $\\theta=\\pi/4$, both coordinates equal $4\\sqrt2/2=2\\sqrt2$. Thus $P=(2\\sqrt2,2\\sqrt2)$."] [17592186092857 "With $r=8$ and $\\theta=240^\\circ$, $$P=(8\\cos240^\\circ,8\\sin240^\\circ)=(-4,-4\\sqrt3).$$"] [17592186092858 "With $r=5$ and $\\theta=330^\\circ$, $$P=(5\\cos330^\\circ,5\\sin330^\\circ)=(\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3}2,-\\tfrac52).$$"] [17592186096968 "Multiplying by $3$ preserves direction and triples length. Multiplying by $-1$ reverses direction and preserves length. The correct diagram therefore has a long vector pointing northeast labeled $3a$, and a vector one-third as long pointing southwest labeled $-a$."] [17592186097030 "Factor the first vector: $4a-12b=4(a-3b)$. Since this common vector is nonzero, equality with $\\lambda(a-3b)$ implies $\\lambda=4$."] [17592186097031 "We have $a-12b=-2(-0.5a+6b)$. Since the vectors are nonzero, equality requires $\\lambda=-2$."] [17592186097032 "The vector $-\\tfrac12a$ has half the length of $a$ and the opposite direction. The original vector points northeast, so the correct diagram shows a vector half as long pointing southwest."] [17592186105459 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2-y) = 2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2-y) = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(2x)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}x({x}^{2}-y)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}({x}^{3}-xy)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue} (x^3-xy) \\sqrt{2+4x^2}}.$"] [17592186105560 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}ab\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{C}$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $b.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =CB=58 \\\\ b & =CA=40 \\\\ m∠C & ={31}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{31^{\\circ}} = 0.515\\,038\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 58\\cdot 40\\operatorname{sin}{31}^{∘} \\\\  & =1160\\cdot (0.515\\hspace{0.167em}038) \\\\  & \\approx 597.44,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186105561 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle ADC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/ac/acd2e923a65d0821cd59ed8b8274ceb5fc38def896467e37c8b1d454f958699e.png)\n\n First, we find the area for $\\triangle ABC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(42)(35)\\operatorname{sin}{36}^{∘} \\\\  & =735\\operatorname{sin}{36}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 432.022\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $\\triangle ADC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ADC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(20)(18)\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘} \\\\  & =180\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 177.265\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD.$ We get\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC}+{\\mathcal{A}}_{ADC} \\\\  & =432.022+177.265 \\\\  & \\approx 609\\end{aligned}$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $609.$"] [17592186105562 "Let's start by finding the area of $\\triangle ACD.$ We get \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot DA\\cdot DC\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(25)(32)\\operatorname{sin}{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =400\\operatorname{sin}{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =375.877... \\\\  & \\approx 376\\hspace{0.167em}{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ and $\\triangle ACD$ gives the area of $\\triangle ABC.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD}-{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =376-300 \\\\  & =76\\hspace{0.167em}{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186105596 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)+4(0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1))+2(0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =-6+4-4 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV = |-6| = 6.\n$$"] [17592186105597 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(-1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)-2(-2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1)+(-2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-3k+6-3 \\\\  & =-3k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ -3k+3 & =0 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=1$."] [17592186105598 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨1,5,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(3\\cdot 0-5\\cdot (-1))-2((-4)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1))-((-4)\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-5-2+23 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ and $\\overrightarrow{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|16|=16.\n$$"] [17592186105691 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)  \n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}\n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\sqrt{2} \\cos   t \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]},\\qquad\nt \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ - 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\sqrt{2} \\sin   t \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\sqrt{2} \\cos   t \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}$ and $y(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t},$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105692 "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) \n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}\n= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\sin   t \\\\ 9 \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}12\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\cos   t \\\\ - 9 \\sin   t\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}9 \\sin   t \\\\ 12 \\cos   t\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+12\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{144{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+81{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106041 "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 24\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step $2.$\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}24\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{24\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+7\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"] [17592186106042 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}}=3,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106061 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}3{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{r}^{3}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106085 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}с & =AB=3, \\\\ b & =AC=7, \\\\ m∠A & ={128}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{128}^{∘}=0.788\\,010,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{128}^{∘} \\\\  & =10.5\\cdot (0.788\\hspace{0.167em}010) \\\\  & \\approx 8.27,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186106086 "The sides adjacent to the $20^\\circ$ angle are $5$ and $13$. The triangle area is $$\\frac12(5)(13)\\sin20^\\circ\\approx11.11565466.$$ Rounded to two decimal places, the area is $11.12$."] [17592186106087 "Draw diagonal $BD$. In $\\triangle ABD$, $$BD^2=10^2+6^2-2(10)(6)\\cos62^\\circ\\approx79.66341.$$ In $\\triangle BCD$, the cosine rule gives $BD^2=9^2+CD^2-18(CD)\\cos51.8^\\circ$. The convex configuration shown selects $CD\\approx11.009953$; the other root gives a concave configuration. Add the two triangle areas: $$[ABCD]=\\tfrac12(10)(6)\\sin62^\\circ+\\tfrac12(9)(11.009953)\\sin51.8^\\circ\\approx65.42354.$$ Rounded to the nearest integer, the area is $65$."] [17592186106088 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16\\cdot 21\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{56}^{∘} \\\\  & =168\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{56}^{∘} \\\\  & =139.278… \\\\  & \\approx 139{\\text{ in}}^{2},\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of  $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}BCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD}-{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =139-90 \\\\  & =49{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186106089 "The large triangle $ABD$ has area $\\tfrac12(12)(9)\\sin117^\\circ\\approx48.11435\\,\\mathrm{in}^2$. The concave quadrilateral is the part remaining after removing $\\triangle BCD$, so $$[BCD]=[ABD]-[ABCD]\\approx48.11435-15=33.11435\\,\\mathrm{in}^2.$$ Rounded to the nearest integer, the answer is $33\\,\\mathrm{in}^2$."] [17592186106094 "Coplanarity means the scalar triple product is zero. Since $b\\times c=\\langle-8,-1,10\\rangle$, we obtain $$a\\cdot(b\\times c)=-8k-1+10=9-8k=0.$$ Hence $k=\\tfrac98$."] [17592186106310 "The volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product. Here $b\\times c=\\langle17,-10,-11\\rangle$, so $$V=|a\\cdot(b\\times c)|=|-3(17)-2(-11)|=|-29|=29.$$"] [17592186106347 "The area formula gives $$\\tfrac12(5)(6)\\sin A=\\tfrac{15\\sqrt3}{2}\\implies\\sin A=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}.$$ The marked angle is acute in the given diagram, selecting $A=60^\\circ$ rather than its obtuse supplement."] [17592186106439 "Split the convex quadrilateral along $BD$ into triangles with included angles $80^\\circ$ and $50^\\circ$. Add their areas: $$[ABCD]=\\tfrac12(5)(8)\\sin80^\\circ+\\tfrac12(10)(7)\\sin50^\\circ\\approx46.50771.$$ Rounded to the nearest tenth, the area is $46.5$."] [17592186114970 "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t))' = \\mathbf f'(t) + 2\\mathbf g'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}+9)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{1}{t-2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}, \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{e}^{3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(8t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+2(4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{e}^{3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =8(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}+6{e}^{3t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-6\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114971 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\big( u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)' = u'(t) \\mathbf{g}(t) + u(t) \\mathbf{g}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $u'(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -t \\\\ 2t{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ -2t \\\\ (2t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114972 "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t) = \\mathbf{f}'(u(t)) u'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}u)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({u}^{2}+5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(4u)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}, \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{u(t)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}){u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{3}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(2{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot 6{t}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+24{t}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+24{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
